        "title": "Reef Diversity: Indo-Pacific vs. Caribbean, Hotspots, Gradients",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Reef biodiversity is not uniformly distributed. The Indo-Pacific contains the overwhelming majority of coral and reef-fish species, with an epicenter in the Coral Triangle. The Caribbean is a separate, impoverished fauna by comparison — the result of geological isolation, extinction events, and a smaller total reef area. Understanding this pattern requires both evolutionary history and ecology."
